// @corpus: platform
// @id: PLATFORM-context
// @category: platform — request context
// @charter: §7.8
// @milestone: E2C
//
// The accessors that introduce a privacy label. Until E2C deleted the
// stand-in, `check.rs` carried a table saying `current_organization()` yields
// `Organization<OrganizationId>` — and the function it described did not
// exist anywhere. The table was the only thing that made the rule work.
//
// Now the label comes from the declared return type, so a function carries a
// restriction because of what it RETURNS, not because of how it is spelled.

module context

// The qualifiers AND the types they qualify. The three id types were missing
// until 2026-08-20: `Session<SessionId>` names `SessionId` and nothing required
// it to resolve, because a privacy label compares written spellings and never
// looked the argument up. Putting the operation's signature on the ABI is what
// forced the question — a label's argument is a real type reference, and the
// two `SessionId` declarations in this program are two different types.
import capability.{ Session, User, Organization, SessionId, UserId, OrganizationId }

// **The platform supplies these.**
//
// Architect ruling, 2026-08-20:
//
// > Host implementation is explicit declaration metadata, never inferred from
// > a missing/`todo` body or from the effect row.
//
// So each carries a `host` binding naming the operation the platform provides,
// and has no body: `todo` means *there is not an implementation here*, and it
// must not secretly mean *the platform supplies an FFI implementation*.
//
// The operation is per FUNCTION, not per capability. `add` and `clear` both
// require `database.write<Carts>` and have different ABIs — which is the model
// error the Wasm encoder found, and the reason a capability may never be a
// callable identity.
fn current_session() -> Session<SessionId> !{ session.read }
    host "pw:host/session#read"

// **Who reads, as the host answers it** (ADR-0263): the session's
// principal's user, or the session's own guest where no one signed in.
// Until ADR-0263 its body was `User("")`, and every reader was one user.
fn current_user() -> User<UserId> !{ session.read }
    host "pw:host/principal#read"

// **The reader's organization, as the host answers it** (ADR-0263). Until
// ADR-0263 its body was `Organization("")`, every reader in one. No host
// answers it yet: a program that asks is refused where it is served.
fn current_organization() -> Organization<OrganizationId> !{ session.read }
    host "pw:host/organization#read"

// **A reader signed in to an account** (track `data-path`): their user's id,
// which names them and grants nothing (ADR-0263, ADR-0270), and the name
// their sign-in provider gave them.
type Account = Account { id: UserId, name: String }

// **The account a reader's handle names** (track `data-path`; what
// ADR-0384's H1 calls `id_of`), for the reader's own handle, as
// `current_user()` answers it, and `None` where no one signed in to an
// account: no one, or a guest, whose user's id holds its session, a
// credential, and is no id to hand a program. The platform's, as the
// principal is (ADR-0338 1.3), so a program that declares its data names its
// reader, and greets them, without a data layer's operation (the feed's
// `users#of-session` and `users#viewer`). Its answer is labelled as the
// handle it is given, so a shared cache refuses it (PW5001).
fn account_of(user: User<UserId>) -> Option<Account> !{ session.read }
    host "pw:host/principal#account"
